[[File:A FARMER CULTIVATES HIS CORN FIELD ON ST. HELENA'S ISLAND - NARA - 546986.jpg|thumb|A man works a cornfield on [[Saint Helena Island (South Carolina)|St. Helena Island]], where "Michael Row the Boat Ashore" was first attested.]]
"'''Michael, Row the Boat Ashore'''" (also called "'''Michael Rowed the Boat Ashore'''", "'''Michael, Row Your Boat Ashore'''", or "'''Michael, Row That Gospel Boat'''") is a traditional [[spirituals|spiritual]] first noted during the [[American Civil War]] at [[Saint Helena Island (South Carolina)|St. Helena Island]], one of the [[Sea Islands]] of [[South Carolina]].<ref>[[William Francis Allen]], [[Charles Pickard Ware]], and Lucy McKim Garrison, [http://docsouth.unc.edu/church/allen/allen.html ''Slave Songs of the United States''], p. xl.</ref> The best-known recording was released in 1960 by the U.S. folk band [[The Highwaymen (folk band)|The Highwaymen]]; that version briefly reached number-one hit status as a single in the United States in September 1961.

